/* GoT wiki long-form archive — places of southern Westeros. Original prose. */
Object.assign(WIKI_EXTRA, {

  // ================= The Crownlands =================

  "loc:kings-landing": {
    paras: [
      "King's Landing began as a boast. Aegon the Conqueror came ashore at the mouth of the Blackwater with three dragons and no particular plan for the muddy hills where his boots first sank, and the wooden fort he raised there — the Aegonfort, all logs and presumption — grew into the largest city in Westeros because power, once planted, waters itself. Three hundred years on, half a million people live stacked on and between the three hills that bear his family's names: the Red Keep glowering from Aegon's High Hill, the Great Sept of Baelor crowning Visenya's, and the broken dome of the Dragonpit moldering on Rhaenys's — a skyline that tells the whole dynasty's story in three buildings, one of them already a ruin when the tale begins.",
      "It is, by every account, a terrible place to live and an irresistible place to be. The stench reaches travelers miles down the roseroad; Flea Bottom's pot-shops serve a brown stew nobody asks the ingredients of; and gold cloaks keep an order that is mostly a price list. Above the squalor the Red Keep runs on a different economy — whispers, marriages, debts and knives — and the genius of the city is how freely the two markets trade. A secret born in a Street of Silk brothel can topple a Hand; a word in the council chamber can empty Flea Bottom into a bread riot. Varys keeps his little birds and Littlefinger his ledgers precisely because in this city information is the only commodity that never spoils.",
      "The story spends more hours inside its walls than anywhere else, and the city repays the attention with catastrophe on a schedule. Ned Stark's honor lasts a single season against it. Joffrey's cruelty, Tyrion's improvised government, the riot that nearly eats the royal party, the Blackwater burning green at the walls — each crisis teaches the same civic lesson, that King's Landing does not belong to whoever holds the throne but to whoever feeds it, frightens it, or fools it last. The Tyrells understand this and arrive with bread; the Sparrows understand it and arrive barefoot; Cersei never learns it, and arms the Faith to spite a rival only to watch the city's piety turn on her own house with chains and bells.",
      "The wildfire beneath the city is its truest metaphor, and both tellings use it twice. Aerys seeded the vaults with the alchemists' green fever, meaning to make his capital a pyre rather than a gift — the order Jaime Lannister killed him to cancel, and the secret he carried in silence while the realm called him oathbreaker. A generation later the caches serve a Lannister after all: the sept's destruction removes a queen's enemies, her trial, and her last child in one green sunrise. When Daenerys finally comes with a dragon, the city that survived every schemer falls in an afternoon — bells ringing surrender into streets already burning — and the ash settles on the oldest question in the tale, whether the fault lay in the queen, in her house, or in the city that taught every ruler it swallowed to choose fear.",
      "What remains at the end is deliberately anticlimactic, and means to be. The throne that organized a continent's ambitions is a puddle of slag; the king is a chronicler who cannot father a dynasty; and the small council reconvenes over mundane business in a half-wrecked keep, arguing about ships and brothels while a broken city gets rebuilt outside the window. King's Landing has outlived the Targaryens, the Baratheons, the Lannisters and its own destruction — the tale's driest verdict on capitals in general, which is that the address survives every argument about who deserves it.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The screen compresses the city's geography — hills flatten, the sept and the keep drift within an easy walk of each other, and the vast sprawl the books describe shrinks or swells as the scene requires — but the deepest divergence is a matter of timing. The show burns the Great Sept of Baelor at the close of its sixth season, wildfire under the floor and the High Sparrow, the queen's uncle and the Tyrells all inside; in the published books the sept still stands, Cersei's walk of atonement has ended not in vengeance but in a trial yet to come, and the Faith Militant remains an unbroken power in the city. Readers know the caches are down there and know whose hand the show put on the candle; whether the books strike the same match, and who burns, is one of the great open questions.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:maidenpool": {
    paras: [
      "Maidenpool is a port town on the Bay of Crabs that had the misfortune to be named for the prettiest legend in Westeros and located on the road of every army in the riverlands. The songs say Florian the Fool first beheld Jonquil bathing here with her sisters, and the pool that made the town's name and fortune still draws the sentimental; House Mooton keeps its seat above the harbor and has learned, over centuries of other people's wars, the river-lord's essential skill of surviving whoever arrives next. The town's walls are modest, its bathhouse famous, and its luck historically poor.",
      "The war years prove the point with a grim thoroughness the books linger on: sacked and burned and sacked again as the fighting sloshes back and forth across the Trident's country, Maidenpool becomes shorthand for what the game of thrones costs the people who never got a seat at the table. Order returns at last under a Reach lord's heavy hand — gallows justice administered from the town's gates, the harbor reopened for a royal wedding's traffic — and travelers bound for the Vale and stranger destinations pass through a town relearning how to be one. Even then the songs persist. That is Maidenpool's quiet argument: the armies always leave, and Florian and Jonquil, absurdly, remain.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:antlers": {
    paras: [
      "The Antlers is the Buckwells' seat in the hills north of King's Landing, close enough to the capital that its lords have made a family tradition of arriving early to swear to whoever seems to be winning. The rack of antlers on their banners is older than the Baratheon stag and the house is touchy on the point, which is the most excitement the seat generally offers. Standing where it does — between the city and the Trident's crossings — the castle has watched most of the realm's armies march past in one direction and rather fewer march back, and has drawn from this the only lesson a small house on a great road can afford: keep the gates in good repair, the granary full, and the oaths flexible.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:rosby": {
    paras: [
      "Rosby is a small castle astride the busy road between Duskendale and King's Landing, which makes its lords wealthier than their walls suggest and their village a place travelers remember chiefly for having passed through it. In the tale its name attaches mostly to Lord Gyles, the coughing fixture of the royal court — a man who attends every council and wedding of the era apparently on the strength of never once being a threat to anyone, and who is eventually handed the crown's treasury on roughly that qualification. The books add a dry postscript: when the cough finally wins, the question of who inherits Rosby's comfortable revenues becomes exactly the sort of small, undignified squabble the realm's great players find time for even with a winter coming — a keep-sized reminder that in the Crownlands, proximity to power is itself an estate.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:dragonstone": {
    paras: [
      "Dragonstone is the piece of Valyria that got away. Raised by dragonlord arts on a smoking island at the mouth of Blackwater Bay, the citadel is stone made to flow — towers shaped as dragons crouching and screaming, gates through fanged mouths, a thousand gargoyles instead of merlons — and no mason alive can say how it was done. When Aenar Targaryen quit the Freehold on the strength of his daughter's dream, twelve years before the Doom proved her right, this grim rock became the last outpost of the greatest civilization in the world; and for a century his descendants perched here, the dragon's egg from which the Conquest hatched. Aegon planned his war in its great hall over the Painted Table, a carved map of Westeros fifty feet long with no borders on it — a piece of furniture that is also a thesis.",
      "As a possession it has always been more symbol than prize. The island is sulfur, fishing villages and obsidian; its revenue would embarrass a middling Reach lord; and the title Prince of Dragonstone became the traditional style of the Targaryen heir precisely because the place's value is dynastic, not agricultural. After the rebellion it goes to Stannis Baratheon, who reads the gift correctly as an insult — the grim rock for the loyal brother, storied Storm's End for the charming one — and fifteen years of that arithmetic shape the man who broods here over Jon Arryn's letters and the truth about Cersei's children. It is on Dragonstone that the red god's fires first burn in Westeros, where the Seven are pulled down on a beach at Melisandre's word, and where a king's war is argued nightly between an onion smuggler's conscience and a priestess's flames.",
      "The story keeps returning to the island because the island keeps mattering more than its garrison. Daenerys was born here in the teeth of the storm that named her, carried off as an infant while the last Targaryen fleet broke on the rocks; when she returns at the head of a new one, the homecoming is staged in silence — a queen touching the sand of a birthplace she cannot remember. From Dragonstone she conducts her Westerosi war, and it is here that Jon Snow comes asking to mine the mountain: the island sits on dragonglass, the one substance besides Valyrian steel that kills the dead, and so the old Valyrian outpost ends the tale as it began — the place where the weapon against the dark is quarried, and where a king and a queen who do not yet know they are kin argue themselves into an alliance.",
      "Its meaning in the larger tale is the one carved into its walls: power that outlives its makers. Every faction that holds Dragonstone — Targaryen, Baratheon, the red god's court, Targaryen again — borrows the same authority from the same black stone, and the stone outlasts each of them. The maesters who catalogue its wonders cannot reproduce them; the smallfolk who fish its waters barely look up at the towers. It is the story's standing reminder that the age of wonders ended, that everyone since has been squatting in its ruins, and that ruins, on the right island, are still worth a war.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "After Stannis sails north the tellings part company over the island's fate. The show simply empties it — the castle stands unclaimed and unexplained for seasons, conveniently vacant for Daenerys's arrival, and her war councils unfold in a redesigned interior whose Chamber of the Painted Table becomes the series' best map room. In the books Dragonstone is a fought-over prize: the crown wants its garrison gone and its symbolism extinguished, and the assault that finally takes it is reported to King's Landing at a terrible price — including, if the reports are true, the ruin by fire and boiling oil of the Reach's most celebrated young knight, a casualty the show never charges because it kept him in the capital to die a different way.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:claw-isle": {
    paras: [
      "Claw Isle sits off Crackclaw Point like a stepping stone to Dragonstone, which is precisely what its lords have always been. The Celtigars carry old Valyrian blood and red crabs on their banners, and for three centuries they made a comfortable career of being the dragon's nearest and most reliable vassals — collecting fine Myrish tapestries, a famous axe, and the mild resentment of their Crackclaw neighbors, who consider the Celtigar tax-gathering visits a form of weather. In the war of the five kings the isle follows Dragonstone to Stannis, as it always follows Dragonstone; the books note what that loyalty costs when the Blackwater goes wrong and an old lord must choose between his king across the sea and his own bed at home. He chooses the bed. Stannis's court calls it treason; nearly everyone else calls it being eighty.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:wickendon": {
    paras: [
      "Wickendon is the Waxleys' quiet seat below the Vale's mountains, on the unfashionable coast where Crackclaw Point's hills run down to the water — a fief of beekeepers, chandlers and salt-farmers whose very house name smells faintly of the trade. The candles of Wickendon light septs a long way from home, which is as close to fame as the place has pursued; its lords have the good sense of small houses on contested borders, swearing up toward the Eyrie or down toward the capital as the century requires and keeping the wax flowing either way. The tale never visits. Wickendon would regard that as the system working.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Reach =================

  "loc:highgarden": {
    paras: [
      "Highgarden sits above the Mander on a green hill in the richest country in Westeros, and looks it: white towers wound with climbing roses, three concentric walls, a briar maze grown as much for beauty as defense, and fields beyond the walls that feed half the realm. This was the seat of the Gardener kings, who ruled the Reach for thousands of years from a throne of living wood until the last of them burned with his host on the Field of Fire. Aegon handed the empty castle to the Gardeners' stewards — and House Tyrell has spent three centuries being magnificently, productively sensitive about the word. Growing Strong, their banners insist; upjumped stewards, their rivals murmur; both are true, which is the joke and the wound at once.",
      "The Tyrell method is the castle made policy. Where the Lannisters mint power and the Starks inherit it, Highgarden grows it — marriages planted like orchards, hospitality as statecraft, and always, underneath the courtesy, the hardest arithmetic in the realm being done by whoever holds the rose. In the tale that is chiefly the Queen of Thorns, who conducts the family's real business from a shaded garden while her amiable son collects titles, and whose two great transactions bracket the war: a granddaughter married to one king in the Reach's own camp, then, when a shadow ends that venture, the same granddaughter delivered to King's Landing with the food that ends a siege and the roses that redecorate a throne room. Highgarden never fields the largest ambitions, only the largest harvest — and the harvest, as the Blackwater proves, is an army.",
      "For most of the story the castle itself stays offstage, a wellspring the plot draws on without visiting — the wagons of bread that make Margaery beloved, the hundred thousand swords everyone is forever courting, the strawberries and sisters Loras name-drops at tourneys. The show finally opens the gates only to sack them: with the Tyrell heirs dead in the sept's ashes, Jaime Lannister and the Tarly levies take the castle almost as an errand, and the last of the roses drinks poison across a table from the man she has been fencing with for seven seasons, settling the score with the most satisfying exit line in the series. The gold of Highgarden pays Lannister debts; the granary of the Reach becomes a war asset; the castle passes, at the end, to a new lord whose qualifications are a hammer and a good heart.",
      "Highgarden's meaning in the tale is the fate of soft power in a hard age. The Tyrells play the game better than nearly anyone — patient, liquid, universally pleasant — and it buys them three royal marriages and a decade at the center of everything; and then a single morning of wildfire deletes the whole position, because charm and wheat have no answer to an enemy who stops caring about the rules. What survives of the rose is what was always realest about it: the fields. Armies burn, queens fall, houses end — the Reach still feeds the realm, whoever collects the rents.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "In the books Highgarden remains unvisited and untaken — a described paradise the reader, like most of Westeros, knows only by reputation, with Olenna alive, Willas and Garlan Tyrell (two brothers the show cut entirely) in the line of succession, and the Reach's real emergency coming not from Lannister columns but from the ironborn: Euron's longships are savaging its coasts and islands, and the rose's strength is marching the wrong way when the fourth book leaves it. The show's swift sack of the castle — and Olenna's poisoned exit with its famous confession — is screen invention from the walls up, efficient, memorable, and impossible in the books' geography, where readers have been promised the Reach's war but not yet shown it.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:old-oak": {
    paras: [
      "Old Oak guards the sea road in the northern Reach, and House Oakheart has held it since the Age of Heroes — their three green leaves on white are among the oldest arms in the south, and their lady in the tale rules a house prouder of its ancientness than its acreage. The seat's chief export has always been knights of the pattern the Reach likes best: brave, courteous, and a little too trusting for the company they keep. The books send the family's most famous son south with a Kingsguard cloak and a princess to protect, into a Dornish intrigue that costs him first his vows and then everything else — a fate Old Oak learns of the way border houses always learn things from Dorne, late and badly. The oak abides; its acorns roll into other people's stories.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:red-lake": {
    paras: [
      "Red Lake sits on the shore of its namesake water in the Reach's far northwest, where House Crane has fished, feasted and married well since the dawn ages. The family claims descent from a daughter of Garth Greenhand — the Rose of Red Lake, who the tales say could become a crane at will, which would explain the sigil and possibly the family's gift for being elsewhere when trouble arrives. The seat watches the marches against the ironborn coast and the westerlands hills, quiet duty in quiet centuries and very much not in loud ones; the tale leaves it in peace, which by Reach standards is a compliment.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:goldengrove": {
    paras: [
      "Goldengrove takes its name from the great golden tree on the Rowan banners, and House Rowan takes its standing from being the sort of family every faction wants and none can take for granted — old blood, broad lands in the northern Reach, and a tradition of level heads in a region that breeds hot ones. In the tale its lord is the Reach's designated reasonable man: present in Renly's pavilions, present at the capital's councils after the Blackwater, valued precisely because his support means the sensible lords are satisfied. Goldengrove itself keeps out of the fighting, which is not luck but the family craft — the Rowans have survived Gardeners, dragons, and every ambitious neighbor by understanding that in the Reach the winning side is usually the one that eats.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:tumbleton": {
    paras: [
      "Tumbleton is a prosperous market town on the upper Mander, close enough to the Blackwater's headwaters to be the Reach's front porch on the Crownlands — wool, wine and river trade under a modest lord, with nothing about it to suggest it once hosted the ugliest chapter of the Dance of Dragons. It did: here the Hightower host camped in the first Dance, here two dragonseed riders sold their queen for promises, and the town paid for the bargain twice over, sacked and burned so thoroughly that 'the treasons of Tumbleton' remains a proverb among people who could not tell you the war it comes from.",
      "The town the tale knows has long since rebuilt over its ashes, the way Mander towns do — the river forgives faster than the histories. Armies still use the road: hosts bound between Highgarden and King's Landing water at Tumbleton as they always have, and its people practice the market town's oldest survival skill, selling to every banner at a fair markup and asking no questions the ale cannot answer. If there is a lesson in the place, the smallfolk learned it a century and a half before the story begins: dragons decide wars, towns absorb them.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:bitterbridge": {
    paras: [
      "Bitterbridge is where the roseroad crosses the Mander on an old stone span, which makes the Caswells' modest castle one of the most strategically placed small seats in the realm: every army moving between Highgarden and King's Landing funnels over their bridge, and the tolls of peace become the headaches of war. The name is said to remember an ancient slaughter on the crossing, and the place has a habit of living up to it — great hosts camp well; great hosts leaving in a hurry do not.",
      "Its hour in the tale comes with Renly's war. The self-crowned king dawdles here with the greatest army Westeros has seen in a generation, holding a melee among the tents to pass the time — and it is at Bitterbridge, in the books, that a broad-shouldered woman from Tarth unhorses the finest knights of the Reach and claims as her boon a place in the king's rainbow-cloaked guard. When the shadow does its work at Storm's End, the vast Tyrell foot is still camped here, suddenly a masterless weapon that every remaining player needs and none can reach first; the scramble for Bitterbridge's leaderless host is the hinge on which the Blackwater — and therefore the war — quietly turns.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:grassy-vale": {
    paras: [
      "Grassy Vale lies at the eastern edge of the Reach where the Mander is young and the border with the stormlands is a matter of opinion, and House Meadows has held it through centuries of that opinion being tested. The name is the least ambitious in Westerosi heraldry and entirely accurate: good grass, fat cattle, a river ford, and lords whose highest boast is that their lands have fed armies they wisely declined to join. In the war years the seat does what the eastern Reach always does — musters its levies for Highgarden's cause and prays the fighting stays on somebody else's grass. Mostly, it does.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:long-table": {
    paras: [
      "Long Table sits on the Mander with a name that is a standing dinner invitation, and House Merryweather has spent generations making sure nobody declines — the groaning boards of Long Table are proverbial in a region that takes feasting seriously as both art and policy. The family's fortunes track the risks of their chosen craft, pleasing the powerful: a Merryweather Hand once paid for a king's failures with exile, and the books bring the house back to court a generation later, the lord amiable at the council table and his Myrish lady rather closer to the queen's ear than is entirely safe for anyone involved. Long Table's lesson is its own furniture — at the realm's feasts, it matters enormously which end of the table you are seated at, and the Merryweathers have sat at both.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:cider-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Cider Hall stands at the meeting of the Mander and the Cockleswhent amid the orchards that put the apple on the Fossoway banners and the house's name on every cask of good cider in the Reach. The family's great schism is the realm's most cheerful feud: at a famous tourney in the age of the hedge knights, one Fossoway's conduct so disgusted his own cousin that the cousin bit into a green apple, declared it his new arms, and founded a rival branch on the spot — and red-apple and green-apple Fossoways have been politely distinguishing themselves from one another ever since. Cider Hall belongs to the red; the green throve on its own lands nearby; and both send their knights to the Reach's wars in numbers, where captains have learned to check which apple before assigning them adjoining tents.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:ashford": {
    paras: [
      "Ashford is a neat little castle and market town on the Cockleswhent in the southern Reach, and it owes its place in the histories to a single summer tourney held generations before the tale begins. The great meadow by the river hosted the most consequential joust ever run: a hedge knight's quarrel with a prince swelled into a trial of seven, and when the dust settled the heir to the Iron Throne lay dead of his own brother's mace, killed defending a landless nobody's cause. Every maester's history of the era pivots on that afternoon at Ashford — the day the realm's future broke its skull for the sake of a poor man's justice, and a boy called Egg took his first steps toward a throne nobody expected him to sit.",
      "The rebellion gave the town a second, harsher entry. Robert Baratheon's whirlwind campaign met its one check at Ashford, where the Reach's best soldier mauled his vanguard and sent the storm king reeling north toward the battle of the Bells and, eventually, the Trident — a defeat that arguably won the war by putting Robert where the war needed him. The town itself keeps its cockleshell quiet either way: fairs on the meadow, tolls at the bridge, and a green field by the river where, if you believe the older smallfolk, a giant of a hedge knight once stood against a prince and the seven watched.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:brightwater-keep": {
    paras: [
      "Brightwater Keep is the seat of House Florent, whose fox-eared banners fly over the best claim to Highgarden that will never be honored. The Florents descend from the Gardener kings by lines they can recite backward, and the fact that Aegon handed the Reach to the Tyrell stewards instead has been the family grievance for three hundred years — nursed at every wedding, aired at every council, and priced into every alliance the foxes make. It sends them, in the tale, to the one king who cares about rightful claims: the Florents are the spine of Stannis's cause, his queen is one of theirs, and Brightwater's levies freeze and burn beside him from the Blackwater to the northern snows.",
      "The bill arrives promptly. Attainted for treason after the Blackwater, the Florents watch their ancient seat handed to a Tyrell son as a wedding favor — the steward's family now granting Gardener land to its own cadets, an irony sharp enough to shave with. The books scatter the foxes to their fates: one uncle to the Wall's justice, another to the fires of the red god's zealots, the rest to exile or the snow. Brightwater Keep itself sits out the wars in comfort, well garrisoned and well tended, waiting to learn which of its claimants history will leave alive.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:bandallon": {
    paras: [
      "Bandallon stands on the Reach's southwestern coast above the Redwyne Straits, where House Blackbar keeps one of the watch-seats that ring the realm's richest sea lane. The straits below carry the Arbor's wine and Oldtown's trade, which means they also carry, in the bad centuries, the longships that come for both — and the Blackbars' purpose has always been to see the sails early and light the fires. It is unglamorous work punctuated by history's worst days, and the tale's later movements bring one of those seasons close: with the ironborn loose under a crow's eye king, the books leave every seat on this coast watching the horizon the old way. Bandallon endures as it always has, a name on the muster rolls and a light on the cliffs.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:honeyholt": {
    paras: [
      "Honeyholt drowses on the Honeywine amid hives and orchards, and House Beesbury has kept it — and the beekeeping that named them both — for longer than most royal lines lasted. Theirs is the gentlest wealth in Westeros: honey, mead, wax for the septs, and a seat close enough to Oldtown to marry into learning as often as land. The family's most famous son was an old man at a council table — master of coin to a dying king, the one voice raised for law and a girl's inheritance on the morning the greens stole a crown, and the first casualty of the Dance of Dragons before it had a name. The accounts disagree on exactly how he died in that chamber; they agree no sword was in his hand. Honeyholt has kept bees and counted honestly ever since, which is perhaps its own kind of memorial.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:horn-hill": {
    paras: [
      "Horn Hill sits in the green foothills where the Reach climbs toward the Red Mountains and Dorne, a fine castle amid terraced gardens and hunting woods — and the seat of House Tarly, whose huntsman banners and Valyrian greatsword Heartsbane announce the family business. The Tarlys are the Reach's soldiers: while the Tyrells feast and the Redwynes sail, Horn Hill breeds the realm's hardest captains, and its lord in the tale is widely held the finest soldier in Westeros, the man who handed Robert Baratheon his only defeat. That reputation is the anvil on which the seat's most famous son was hammered — a bookish, gentle, unforgivably unwarlike heir given a choice beneath these hills that no father should offer: the black of the Wall, or a hunting accident with his name on it.",
      "The show brings Samwell back through the gates in the story's late years, a Sweetrobe of the Citadel with a wildling woman and a baby at his side, and stages the visit as the dinner from hell — his mother's warmth, his father's contempt served course by course, and a theft on the way out that is also a verdict: Heartsbane leaves Horn Hill under the arm of the son judged unfit to hold it. The sword goes on to do exactly what a Tarly blade should, opening dead things in the Long Night while the men who kept the name die in a war of thrones — burned for a stubbornness their house would have called honor. The estate ends the tale as books' readers suspect it must eventually: in the hands of the soft son, whose weapons were the only ones that turned out to matter.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The books never visit Horn Hill; it exists entirely in Samwell's memories, which are worse than the show's version, not better — a childhood of chains in the yard and cruelties dressed as cures, sketched in asides that make Randyll Tarly one of the most chilling fathers in the story without a single scene at home. The show's dinner, Gilly's quiet defiance, and the taking of Heartsbane are all screen inventions, as are the Tarlys' deaths by dragonfire; in the books Randyll is very much alive, holding Maidenpool for the crown and dispensing his iron justice, with Dickon at his side and the family sword still over his hearth.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:oldtown": {
    paras: [
      "Oldtown is the city King's Landing wishes it were: older than the Andals, cleaner than anywhere, and lovelier by every account than any other city in Westeros — cobbled streets, stone guildhalls, and the Honeywine winding through beneath bridges built when Valyria still stood. No one knows who raised the fused black stone fortress on Battle Isle that the Hightower now stands upon; the maesters have theories, the theories have footnotes, and the footnotes disagree, which locals find fitting for a city whose principal export is learned argument. The Hightower itself — beacon, palace and sigil in one — has guided ships up Whispering Sound for so long that House Hightower's motto is simply what the building does: We Light the Way.",
      "Two institutions make Oldtown the realm's second capital, and the quieter power of the two is not the one with the swords. The Starry Sept was the seat of the Faith for a thousand years — it was here a High Septon anointed Aegon the Conqueror, in the bargain that saved the city from the fate of Harrenhal — until the office followed power to King's Landing. The Citadel never followed anyone. The maesters are forged here, chain by chain, and dispatched to every keep in Westeros to heal, teach, count and advise; which means the order's quiet opinions — about magic, about dragons, about what a rational realm should contain — travel out with every grey robe, and have been shaping the Seven Kingdoms for centuries from a city most kings never visit.",
      "The tale mostly feels Oldtown at a distance — every maester at every castle is its ambassador, from Luwin's patient lessons to Pycelle's calculated doddering — until Samwell Tarly is sent to become one, and the story finally walks the Citadel's halls. The show plays the visit for scale and comedy first: the Sweeping shot of the library's chained galleries, archmaesters serenely uninterested in the apocalypse, and Sam doing the actual work — curing a greyscale case the experts had written off, thieving restricted books, and deducing more from marginalia than the order has from centuries of ravens. The joke has a sharp point. The realm's institution of knowledge, faced with the return of everything it catalogued as legend, files the warning and asks for the dishes to be cleared.",
      "The books' Citadel is stranger and darker. Sam arrives at the end of a long sea road to find the city already humming with wrongness — a novice's dead body politic, glass candles burning that should not burn, an archmaester of the occult who says out loud what readers have long suspected: that the order's rationalism may be less a temperament than a policy, and that the dragons did not merely die. Between the marble and the mysteries, Oldtown closes the fourth book as the tale's most loaded unfired weapon, with the ironborn's mad king sailing for the Whispering Sound and the city of light straining to pretend the age of wonders is not coming back up its river.",
      "What Oldtown means in the story is memory — the realm's long memory, professionally kept, selectively edited. Winterfell remembers with crypts and Oldtown with catalogues, and the tale trusts neither completely: the Citadel's archives hold the answer to nearly every riddle the plot poses, and the order's genius has been deciding which answers the realm is ready to hear. The beacon on the Hightower has burned through every one of the realm's dark ages. What the city underneath it chooses to illuminate is another matter.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show's Oldtown is essentially the Citadel with a skyline — no Starry Sept, no Hightower politics, no sense of the city as the Faith's old heart — and Sam's stay is compressed to a season of montage before he abandons the chain for the war. The books give the city a full point-of-view life the show never touches: the prologue murder among the novices, Marwyn the Mage and his glass candle, a Dornish sphinx studying under a false name, Hightower intrigues in the tower's high chambers, and Euron Greyjoy's fleet bearing down on the Whispering Sound with intentions the sample chapters make sound less like conquest than ritual. Show-Oldtown is where Sam learns the dead walk; book-Oldtown is shaping up to be where the war for the dawn has a second front.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:blackcrown": {
    paras: [
      "Blackcrown holds the Reach's southwestern corner past Oldtown, where the land runs out and the sea takes over the argument, and House Bulwer has kept it since the days when that corner needed keeping against longships and worse. It is a seat of cliffs, herds and hard weather off the water, sworn to the Hightowers as all this coast is, and its levies march when Oldtown calls. The tale touches it only in the margins the books keep for such places — a house led, in the war years, by a child lady, which is how the era's arithmetic works out for many small seats once the muster rolls come home short. Blackcrown's crown stays on; the heads beneath it change too often.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:uplands": {
    paras: [
      "Uplands sits on the high ground above the Honeywine valley with Oldtown's towers in view on a clear day, and House Mullendore has held it in comfortable obscurity for as long as anyone has bothered recording — a seat of vineyards, slopes and mild autumns, sworn to the Hightowers and rarely asked for more than its tithes and a respectable showing at tourneys. Its knights ride in the Reach's wars because the Reach's wars are compulsory for gentlemen, and its lords come home when they can; one of them left pieces of himself on the Blackwater, the books note in passing, which is more history than Uplands usually consents to make. The place's true vocation is the view, and it has never apologized for that.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:three-towers": {
    paras: [
      "Three Towers is exactly what it says: three joined keeps on the southern arm of the Whispering Sound, watching the sea approaches to Oldtown from the far shore, held by House Costayne of the old First Man blood. The Costaynes have been kings' companions, admirals and the occasional traitor across the Reach's long history — old enough to have married into everything and quarreled with most of it — and their seat's business has always been the water: what comes up the Sound reaches Oldtown past Three Towers first. In quiet centuries that means fishing fleets and wine ships. The tale's late chapters, with the ironborn loose in the southern seas, make the watch suddenly current again; the towers were built for exactly the horizon their lords now scan.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:sun-house": {
    paras: [
      "Sun House takes the Reach's southern shore full in the face — a bright seat above warm water where House Cuy has held the coast between Oldtown's hinterland and the Summer Sea since the old kings' days. The name is the climate and the climate is the estate: early harvests, long sailing seasons, and a port-side prosperity that never quite amounts to power, which suits the Cuys, whose ambitions have historically extended to good marriages and better weather. Their knights turn up in the Reach's hosts wherever the Hightowers point them, and the books lose one in the usual place, the Blackwater, where the south's flower went to be pruned. Sun House grieves in sunlight and carries on; the coast permits no other style.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Stormlands =================

  "loc:storms-end": {
    paras: [
      "Storm's End is a dare flung at the sea. The great drum tower rises from cliffs above Shipbreaker Bay, its seaward wall a hundred feet of doubled stone, and the gales that give the bay its name break against it as they have for thousands of years — because, the songs say, the castle was built to spite a god. Durran Godsgrief loved a daughter of the sea and sky, and when her divine parents drowned his wedding guests he answered with fortresses, six of which the storms tore down; the seventh stands, raised (some tales insist) with the counsel of a boy who would be called Bran the Builder, and woven — Melisandre herself senses it — with old spells in the very stones. Whatever the truth, the facts are the legend's best argument: Storm's End has never fallen. Not to storm, not to siege, not to anything that came at its walls honestly.",
      "The castle's history is the Baratheon founding myth in stone. Aegon's fierce bastard-born general Orys slew the last Storm King, took his daughter, his words and his banners, and planted the crowned stag here; every Baratheon since has been measured against those walls' stubbornness, and the three brothers of the tale each inherited a different piece of it. It was Stannis who gave the place its modern legend, holding it through a year of the rebellion's siege while the Tyrell host feasted within sight of the starving garrison — a defense that ran to rats and boot leather, was saved by an onion smuggler's midnight run through the cordon, and forged both the grimmest lord in Westeros and his most honest servant. Robert's reward for that year was to give the castle to Renly, a wound Stannis files under justice and never stops paying interest on.",
      "The war of the five kings puts the castle at the center of its strangest hour. Renly dies at its edge with his hundred thousand swords camped around him; the storm lords kneel to Stannis over his brother's body; and the castellan who refuses to yield the castle itself learns that these walls, proof against every army, have no answer to what Melisandre carries in her. The thing Davos ferries beneath Storm's End by night — and watches, appalled, being born — is the tale's clearest statement that the old defenses guard against the old threats only. The castle never falls, and it changes hands anyway; the distinction between those two sentences is where the whole war lives.",
      "The endgame returns the stag to its rock from an unexpected direction. Robert's last unacknowledged son, a smith from Flea Bottom rowed out of two executions, is legitimized with a stroke and given the seat of his father's fathers — the show's tidiest piece of dynastic housekeeping, a Baratheon at Storm's End again by way of an armorer's bench. The books are playing a longer and stranger game with the same square: another of Robert's bastards is hidden abroad, the castle's garrison holds for a king gone north, and the last chapters published leave professional exiles with a dragon banner and a young man who may or may not be Rhaegar's son taking the unfallen castle at last — by escalade and audacity, from a garrison of dozens, footnote-style, as if the story wanted the record kept technically intact.",
      "What Storm's End means in the tale is endurance as identity — and its limits. The castle embodies the Baratheon words made architecture, fury answered with permanence; and the story spends its length demonstrating that permanence is not the same as posterity. The walls outlast the storms. Whether the house does is the question the tale keeps asking under every crowned stag, and the answer, in both tellings, comes down not to stone but to a boy nobody planned for.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings diverge underneath the castle. The show gives Melisandre one shadow, born in a cave on the shore, and spends it on Renly; the books give her two — the first conceived for Renly, the second birthed directly beneath Storm's End to kill the castellan Cortnay Penrose, with Davos rowing witness and the castle's ancient wardings the reason the horror must be delivered from inside. The books also keep the castle strategically alive long after the show forgets it: Edric Storm, Robert's bastard, is garrisoned and smuggled from there in place of the show's Gendry arc, and the Golden Company's capture of Storm's End for young Aegon's cause — a claimant the show cut entirely — sets up a battle at its walls that the published books leave impending. On screen the castle is an heirloom awaiting Gendry; on the page it is a loaded position in a war still being played.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:summerhall": {
    paras: [
      "Summerhall was the Targaryens' argument that the dragon could rest — not a fortress but a pleasure palace, lightly walled and full of music, raised in the Dornish Marches to celebrate the peace that finally brought Dorne into the realm. For three generations it was the family's soft place: summers away from the capital's stink, harpists and weddings, the court of a king who preferred it to his throne. That the Targaryens built exactly one castle for joy, and that it is the one that burned, is the kind of symmetry the family's history deals in.",
      "What happened on the night it died is the saga's most carefully kept secret. A king was there — the old hero of Ashford Meadow, grown grey chasing the prophecy of the dragon reborn — with eggs, wildfire, or hope, depending on which whispered account you trust; the fragments the books allow are burned pages and grief. King Aegon the Fifth died in the fire with his dearest, the realm lost the best of its Kingsguard trying to save them, and on that same night, amid the smoke and lamentation, Rhaegar Targaryen was born — a prince conceived in a palace of summer and delivered from its pyre, who grew up haunted by the place, riding out with his harp to sleep in its ruins and returning with songs that made maidens weep. Whatever prophecy died at Summerhall, Rhaegar clearly believed he had inherited it, and the tale's whole tragedy — Harrenhal, Lyanna, the Trident — runs downhill from that belief.",
      "The ruin keeps a second, smaller history: it was among these blackened stones that Robert Baratheon won three battles in a single day at his rebellion's start, the storm lord announcing himself on the Targaryens' field of grief. And far to the north, an old blind maester at the Wall carries the palace in his name and never speaks of it — Aemon of House Targaryen, who refused the crown that went to the brother who burned. Summerhall is the tale's quietest ruin: no ghosts anyone will swear to, only the certainty that the family that dreamed of dragons finally called one up, and it was fire, and it was grief.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:griffins-roost": {
    paras: [
      "Griffin's Roost stands on a headland of red stone thrust into Shipbreaker Bay, reached by a narrow neck its household can hold against an army — a proud small seat for a proud small house, whose griffin banners flew highest for one generation exactly. Jon Connington was Prince Rhaegar's dearest friend, made Hand of a failing king in the rebellion's desperate hour, and broke his career at the battle of the Bells: too honorable to burn a town to smoke out Robert Baratheon, he lost the battle, the Handship and his home, exiled while lesser men kept their chairs. The Roost was stripped of most of its lands for his failure and handed down a diminished line — a castle punished for its lord's insufficient ruthlessness, which is as stormlands a fate as exists.",
      "The books bring the griffin home silver-haired and grimmer. Connington returns from exile with the Golden Company at his back and a young man he calls Rhaegar's son at his side, and takes his own castle by stealth and memory — the first move of a claimant's war the show never adapts. He climbs to the roof where he once dreamed, notes how small the great keep of his childhood has become, and carries with him, hidden beneath a glove, the grey death he caught saving a dwarf from drowning. Griffin's Roost thus holds one of the saga's live fuses: a father's love, a pretender's cause, and greyscale, all garrisoned behind one narrow neck of red stone.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:rain-house": {
    paras: [
      "The Rain House earns its name daily — it stands at the wet edge of Cape Wrath where the rainwood meets the sea, and House Wylde has been drying out beside its fires since the Storm Kings' time. It is a seat of timber wealth, hard sailors and harder weather, whose lords have a family tradition of serving crowns as soldiers and officers of the law; the era of the Dance knew a Wylde as the Ironrod, a master of laws so rigid about male succession that he ended up an argument for the other side. In the tale's wars the house follows the storm lords' usual road — Renly first, then Stannis, then wherever defeat scatters a region's oaths. The rain, at least, is reliable.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:stonehelm": {
    paras: [
      "Stonehelm commands the river Slayne where the stormlands shade into the Dornish Marches, and House Swann has held it — and its bridge, and its tolls, and its grudges — through a thousand years of border war. Their arms are two swans, black and white on opposing halves, and no sigil in Westeros is more honest: the Swanns are famous for producing sons on both sides of every question, a marcher house's actuarial approach to civil war. The tale bears the pattern out — one Swann son wears a Kingsguard's white through three kings' worth of chaos while the family's allegiance at home drifts with the weather — and Stonehelm sails on regardless, the way it has outlasted Storm Kings, Dornish spears and dragonlords alike. Some swans are black and some are white, the family likes to observe; all of them are Swanns.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:mistwood": {
    paras: [
      "Mistwood sits deep in the rainwood of Cape Wrath, where the sea fogs roll inland through trees old enough to remember the children of the forest, and House Mertyns keeps a seat more heard of than seen — which its neighbors will tell you is also true of the castle itself on most mornings. It is a lordship of timber, game and solitude, its banners carrying the white owl of a house that prefers watching to being watched. The tale's wars pull its levies out of the trees like everyone else's — storm lords have no neutrality to sell — but Mistwood's real allegiance has always been to the wood, the wet, and the long views of a house that measures history in trees rather than kings.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:blackhaven": {
    paras: [
      "Blackhaven guards the Boneway where it climbs out of the stormlands toward Dorne, a strong dark-stoned castle in the driest, most fought-over corner of the Marches, and House Dondarrion has held the pass for centuries under the strangest arms in the region: forked purple lightning on black, remembering the storm-sent bolt that saved the house's founder from Dornish ambush and struck down his attacker in the road. Marcher lords are bred to a particular temper — vigilant, hospitable and quick — and the Dondarrions have supplied the realm with model marchers for generations, along with squires from Dorne itself, the marches' old wars having mellowed into the marches' old marriages.",
      "The tale empties the castle with a single errand. Its young lord rides to King's Landing for a tourney, catches the eye of half the court, and is handed a commission by Ned Stark: take a hundred men and bring the Mountain to justice. The lightning lord who rides out never truly comes home — what returns to the riverlands' war, again and again, is something the red god keeps relighting, a leader of broken men fighting for the smallfolk long after his own memories gutter. Blackhaven waits meanwhile at the top of its pass, a seat whose lord is legally alive, factually otherwise, and in both tellings the tale's foremost proof that death, for some purposes, is negotiable.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:haystack-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Haystack Hall is the most cheerfully named castle in the stormlands, seat of House Errol amid the good farmland north of Storm's End, and the name is honest labor: this is the region's grain country, hayricks to the horizon in autumn, and the Errols' banners simply admit what the family fortune is built on. The stormlands like to pretend they are all cliffs and thunder; Haystack Hall is the pantry that pretension eats from. Its lords muster with Storm's End in every war the tale runs through — Renly's great host, then Stannis's grim remnant — and go home, those who do, to the harvest, which unlike kings has never once failed to come back.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:felwood": {
    paras: [
      "Felwood sits in the green country where the kingswood's southern eaves shade into stormlands soil, a woodland seat of House Fell — huntsmen, foresters and border knights whose lands have the mixed blessing of lying on the road between King's Landing and Storm's End. Armies moving between the two capitals of the tale's first war pass Felwood's gates going both directions, and its lords practice the roadside house's arithmetic of hospitality and hedged oaths. The books send the family's blood north with Stannis into the snows, where the house's old grudge against the Boltons — a Fell lord killed by a Bolton's hand in an earlier age — rides along like an heirloom. The wood abides; the fells of House Fell are mostly elsewhere, which is the stormlands way.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:bronzegate": {
    paras: [
      "Bronzegate takes its name from the ancient bronze-bound gates in its walls, said to be older than the castle around them, and House Buckler has kept both on the road from the capital to Storm's End since the Storm Kings ruled. It is the classic seat of the second rank — strong enough to matter, never strong enough to be the story — and its lords have made a craft of that station: Bucklers ride in every Baratheon host, hold the door in every Baratheon retreat, and appear in the histories almost exclusively in lists of the loyal. In the tale their banners follow Renly's crown and then Stannis's cause, absorbing the era's defeats with the patience of a house whose gates have been promising, in bronze, to outlast everything, and so far keeping their word.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:evenfall-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Evenfall Hall is the seat of House Tarth on the island the songs call the Sapphire Isle — for the astonishing blue of its waters, not for gems, a distinction on which a great deal of the tale's best subplot ends up hanging. Tarth sits in the narrow sea off the stormlands coast, mountains and waterfalls and pastures folded into an island lovely enough that even Westerosi cynics concede the point, and its lords — styled the Evenstar, a title older than memory — claim descent from both Storm Kings and, more improbably, the Durrandon line's divine grief. It is a small, old, honorable house at the edge of everything, which turns out to be exactly the soil that grows its famous daughter.",
      "Brienne is Evenfall's only living heir — a lonely girl who outgrew every suitor her father found, was mocked at the dances the island threw for her, and left to follow a king who had once, at one of those dances, simply been kind. The island's name saves her life on the road: her captor spares her from rape and mutilation partly on the strength of a lie about sapphires, coined from her home's misnomer. Evenfall Hall itself stays offstage in both tellings — an aging Evenstar keeping a hall short one daughter — but it is the standing answer to anyone puzzled by Brienne's unfashionable decency. Somewhere off the stormlands coast there is an island where the water is impossibly blue and oaths are still taken literally; she is what it exports.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:nightsong": {
    paras: [
      "Nightsong holds a pass through the Dornish Marches for House Caron, whose nightingale banners and title — Lords of the Marches — announce the family's two inheritances, music and war, in the order the marches permit. Seat of harpers and hard riders both, Nightsong spent centuries as Dorne's first argument and the stormlands' first answer, and its lords grew famous for excelling at everything a marcher must: the sword, the wall, the song after. The tale takes its young lord to Renly's rainbow-cloaked guard and then to the Blackwater, where the house's luck runs out in single combat at the walls — and the books hand the ancient seat, with the era's bleak humor, to the minor knight who did the killing, a new-made marcher lord learning that the nightingales come with the mortgage. Dorne, as ever, is watching from the pass.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= Dorne =================

  "loc:sunspear": {
    paras: [
      "Sunspear rises at the desert's eastern edge where Dorne meets the sea — less a castle than an argument between two peoples, resolved in sandstone. The old Martell keep got its slender Spear Tower and the great dome of the Tower of the Sun when Nymeria of the Rhoyne landed her ten thousand ships, wed Mors Martell, and burned the fleet so no one could suggest going back; the banners over the gates quarter her sun with his spear, and the house that rules from beneath them styles its head neither king nor lord but Prince, in the Rhoynish fashion. Around the castle's walls huddles the shadow city — winding mud-brick alleys, bazaars and the long climb of the Winding Walk — because Dorne's capital grew like Dorne itself: not planned, not conquered, accreted out of heat and stubbornness.",
      "The house words are the region's history compressed: Unbowed, Unbent, Unbroken. Alone of the Seven Kingdoms, Dorne never fell to the dragons — Aegon's conquest broke against sand, thirst and a war without battles, and a queen's dragon fell from the sky over the Hellholt to prove the point. Dorne came into the realm a century later by marriage, on terms, keeping its princes and its laws, and Sunspear has never let the Iron Throne forget the difference between a kingdom conquered and a kingdom courted. The pride cuts both ways: it was a Princess of Dorne, Elia Martell, who married the last dragon prince and died with her children in the sack of King's Landing — the wound beneath every Martell scene in the tale, nursed for seventeen years in the Water Gardens' shade.",
      "The tale's Sunspear is a court of concealed motion. Prince Doran, gout-ridden and unreadable, rules from his rolling chair with a patience his own subjects mistake for surrender; his brother Oberyn is the family's public temper, the Red Viper who rides north to the capital for a wedding, a trial, and the duel that reopens every old wound at once. The fury that follows — the shadow city clamoring for war, the prince's own blood plotting around him — is the books' great Dornish movement: a paralyzed ruler revealed, in the tale's most quietly devastating reversal, to have been playing the longest game on the board, his children seeded toward crowns and his enemies toward each other. Vengeance, justice — the old prince's whisper names the price of seventeen years of stillness, and the books leave Dorne cocked like a crossbow.",
      "In the show, Sunspear's story is briefer and bloodier: the viper's paramour and daughters answer Oberyn's death by murdering the peace — a poisoned kiss for a princess, a blade for the prince who counseled patience — and Dorne enters the great war as a faction of vengeance under no banner but grief, spending itself in a queen's cause far from home. Between the tellings, one truth holds: Sunspear is the capital of the realm's one unconquered people, and the tale uses it to ask its most Dornish question — whether patience is wisdom or just slow anger, and whether there is a difference once the spear finally flies.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "No region divides the tellings like Dorne. The books build Sunspear a full political world — Doran's decade-spanning plot to marry his children into a Targaryen restoration, Arianne's queenmaker scheme with Myrcella, Quentyn's doomed errand east, the Sand Snakes as distinct dangerous women dispatched on separate missions — all narrated with the region's own point-of-view chapters. The show compresses this to a revenge plot: Doran is a genial obstacle removed in a single scene, Trystane with him, and Ellaria and three interchangeable Sand Snakes seize Dorne offscreen, then dissolve into Daenerys's war. Book readers regard the adaptation of Dorne as the show's most complete demolition; even the show's partisans mostly grant the point, and the books' Dornish game — still unresolved — remains one of the strongest reasons the two tellings will end by different roads.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:vultures-roost": {
    paras: [
      "The Vulture's Roost is a broken eyrie in the western Red Mountains, and its ruin is a Dornish history lesson: this was the perch of the Vulture Kings, the outlaw claimants who gathered broken men and border grudges in the high passes and bled the marches for generations between Dorne's wars. Kingsgrave and Blackmont hunted them; storm lords and Reach lords burned them out; and every burning, the marchers grumble, only scattered the seeds for the next flock. The Roost has stood empty since the last of them, a shell of dry stone on a crag that commands three valleys — which is precisely why no one quite believes it will stay empty. In the mountains of Dorne, a ruin with a view is just a throne between claimants.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:wyl": {
    paras: [
      "Wyl clings to the Boneway where the pass narrows worst, and House Wyl has held it through every war the marches ever fed — often as the war's most enthusiastic participant. The border ballads about the Wyls are the kind sung with the doors barred: in the old fighting against the Young Dragon's conquest, the lords of Wyl earned a name for cruelties that even Dorne, which reveres its resistance, recounts with a wince rather than a toast. Their seat's business is the pass itself — tolls in peacetime, ambush in wartime, the Boneway's bones being partly their work — and the tale leaves them at that ancient trade, a small house whose entire history is a narrow road and what they were willing to do on it.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:kingsgrave": {
    paras: [
      "Kingsgrave stands high in the Prince's Pass with the bleakest name and the cheerfulest lords in the Red Mountains — House Manwoody, whose banners carry a crowned white skull on black in open commemoration of the king their seat is named for: a crowned invader, the tales say, slain at their gates, his rank no help at the altitude. The Manwoodys are that Dornish paradox, a family of wits and courtiers holding a fortress of grim purpose; their sons turn up at Sunspear's court and in princes' escorts, quick with a jape, while their castle goes on doing what it was built for — reminding traffic in the pass that Dorne buried a king once and kept the receipt.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:blackmont": {
    paras: [
      "Blackmont rises above the Torentine in Dorne's far west, where the river runs young and cold out of the Red Mountains, and House Blackmont flies a vulture over it — marcher heraldry of the unapologetic school, remembering centuries of raid and counter-raid against the Reach and the stormlands in which the Blackmonts were seldom the raided. Theirs is old First Man blood turned thoroughly Dornish, holders of one of the western passes and neighbors to the Vulture Kings' old country, whose outlaw flocks they alternately fought and, their enemies claimed, fed. The tale finds the house where Dornish houses are found — a lady of Blackmont in the prince's councils, spears ready in the pass — waiting, like all Dorne, for the word from Sunspear that patience is over.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:skyreach": {
    paras: [
      "Skyreach is the Fowlers' eyrie at the Dornish end of the Prince's Pass, towers stacked up a mountainside so that the seat seems less built than perched — fitting for a house whose banners fly a hooded hawk and whose words are an open dare: Let Me Soar. The Fowlers were Kings of Stone and Sky before Nymeria came, and made the shrewdest bargain of the conquest era, backing the Rhoynish princess early and keeping their pass, their pride and the title Warden of the Prince's Pass as the price of a knee. The tale's Fowlers keep the old franchise — the Old Hawk holding the western door of Dorne, twin daughters running with the Martells' restless younger generation — and the pass keeps its ancient meaning: when Dorne finally moves, this is one of the two roads it moves by, and the hawks see everything on it first.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:yronwood": {
    paras: [
      "Yronwood commands the Boneway's Dornish mouth, and House Yronwood has never entirely forgiven history for the Martells. They were the greatest kings in Dorne before Nymeria's ships came — the Bloodroyal, a title their lords still carry, with lands and levies second only to Sunspear's even now — and the family's ten-thousand-year grudge is the load-bearing tension of Dornish politics: every rebellion Dorne ever brewed internally had an Yronwood in it somewhere, and every Martell prince learns young that the realm's second house must be honored, watched and never quite trusted, in that order.",
      "The tale's era opens with the feud freshly bandaged. A young Oberyn Martell dueled the Lord Yronwood of his day over an indiscretion — first blood only, by the forms, except the lord's wounds festered and killed him, and Dorne has whispered about the Viper's blades ever since. The peace price was a Martell heir fostered at Yronwood, and the books make the arrangement genuinely warm: Quentyn Martell grows up more Yronwood than Martell, and when he rides east on his father's desperate dragon errand, it is Yronwood sons who go with him and pay the road's tolls in blood. The Bloodroyal's loyalty, the books quietly note, is once again collateral in a Martell gamble — and House Yronwood has a long memory for the arithmetic of who owes whom.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:starfall": {
    paras: [
      "Starfall stands where the Torentine meets the Summer Sea in Dorne's far west, a pale castle on an island at the river's mouth, and House Dayne has held it since the Dawn Age — which at Starfall is not a figure of speech. The family treasure is Dawn, a greatsword pale as milkglass, forged (the tale insists, and for once no maester argues) from the heart of a fallen star; and the house's peculiar honor is that the blade is not the lord's by right. Dawn waits, sometimes for generations, until a knight of the house is judged worthy of it, and only then is he named the Sword of the Morning — the realm's one title that must be earned twice, by blood and by soul.",
      "The tale's shadow over Starfall is the last man to bear it. Ser Arthur Dayne was the finest knight of Aerys's Kingsguard and, by the accounting of the man who killed him, the finest sword he ever faced; he died at a tower in the Red Mountains defending a dying king's secret, and Ned Stark — bleeding, grieving, and owing his life to a crannogman's knife in the back of the duel — rode to Starfall afterward to return Dawn to the family. What else passed at Starfall that day the books keep folded: Lady Ashara Dayne, beauty of a generation and a name entangled with the Starks at Harrenhal's great tourney, threw herself into the sea from the castle's seaward tower, and the hows and whys of it are among the saga's most carefully hoarded silences. The sword came home; the Sword of the Morning has not been named since; and Starfall keeps its griefs the way it keeps Dawn — waiting for someone worthy of them.",
      "The house endures in the tale's margins with a poignancy few great houses manage. Its young lord, another Ned, squires in the riverlands for the lightning lord's broken men and speaks of milk-brothers and old loyalties that hint how tangled the Dayne and Stark stories truly are; a cousin called Darkstar carries the family's edge without its honor through the books' Dornish intrigues. Starfall itself never hosts a battle or a wedding on the page — it does not need to. It is the tale's chapel of the old chivalry: the place where the best knight came from, where his sword sleeps, and where the story keeps its unpaid debts to the age of honor filed under a falling star.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The show never sails to Starfall; House Dayne exists on screen only as Ser Arthur at the tower — famously restaged with a sword in each hand, where book readers expected the pale greatsword Dawn, an omission partisans still relitigate. The books keep the house threaded through everything: Edric Dayne's quiet revelations about Wylla and the Stark connection, Ashara's death as an open mystery with rival explanations, Darkstar loose in the Dornish plot, and Dawn itself waiting at Starfall — an unclaimed sword that readers, noting what the blade is made of and what is coming from the north, refuse to believe the author has finished with.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:hellholt": {
    paras: [
      "The Hellholt squats on the Brimstone in the deep Dornish desert, a grim strong castle on a sulfurous river, and House Uller wears its reputation like armor: half the Ullers are half-mad, the Dornish saying runs, and the other half are worse. The saying is affectionate, mostly. The Ullers are fierce, loyal to Sunspear beyond question, and famous for a hospitality no one with a grievance against them should accept — their history includes at least one feast that ended with the doors barred and the hall burning, which is why Dornishmen decline invitations to the Hellholt politely and from a distance.",
      "The castle's great day is Dorne's greatest: during the Conqueror's Dornish war, it was above the Hellholt that a defender's iron scorpion bolt found the eye of the dragon Meraxes, and queen and dragon came down together in ruin — the only dragon the Targaryens lost in all their conquests, and proof written in the sand that Dorne could kill gods. What became of Queen Rhaenys herself the accounts refuse to settle, and the darker versions are told, naturally, about the Hellholt. In the tale's present the house's fire burns in Ellaria Sand, an Uller lord's natural daughter, paramour of the Red Viper and — after the Mountain's hands close that story — the voice demanding Dorne answer blood with blood. The Brimstone runs yellow and stinking past the walls; nobody at the Hellholt has ever pretended the water was sweet.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:sandstone": {
    paras: [
      "Sandstone lies a week's ride into the deep desert west of the Brimstone, and House Qorgyle — three black scorpions on red — holds the driest lordship in the Seven Kingdoms from a castle the color of its country. Life at Sandstone is wells, dates and distance; its levies are desert riders the equal of any in Dorne; and its house has a knack for turning up at history's odd corners — a Qorgyle commanded the Night's Watch a generation before the tale, and a Qorgyle son stood among the companions who followed the Red Viper north to King's Landing and his duel. The scorpions on the banners are the family's climate, character and foreign policy in one image: unglamorous, patient, and inadvisable to step on.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:the-tor": {
    paras: [
      "The Tor stands on the Sea of Dorne where the coast bends green and mild — by Dornish standards nearly lush — and House Jordayne rules the pleasantest lordship in the principality from a seat named with desert bluntness for the hill it sits on. The Jordaynes are the salty Dornish of the coasts, more traders and readers than raiders; their lord in the tale is a man of letters whose heir is his daughter, in the Dornish way that puts inheritance before chromosomes and scandalizes the other six kingdoms. The Tor's role in the wars is the coastal seat's eternal one — ships, grain and watchfulness on the sea approaches to Sunspear — performed with the mild superiority of a house whose lands the desert never quite reached.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:ghost-hill": {
    paras: [
      "Ghost Hill rises chalk-white above the Sea of Dorne, and House Toland flies over it the strangest banner in the principality: a green dragon devouring its own tail. The arms are a jape three centuries old — during the Conquest, the tale runs, the Lord Toland sent his champion out to face Aegon the Conqueror in single combat, and the dragon king killed the man before learning he was the castle's fool, sent to make exactly the point the Tolands then fixed in their heraldry: the dragon chases itself, time devours its own works, and Dorne can wait. The hill's name is older, kept for the ghost said to walk the white keep, and the Tolands, who have buried a fool and outlasted a dynasty, see no reason to evict either the ghost or the joke. In the books the house's ladies stand close in Sunspear's councils when the long game finally quickens — patience, at Ghost Hill, being both the sigil and the strategy.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:godsgrace": {
    paras: [
      "Godsgrace sits where the Greenblood gathers its two branches, in the richest soil Dorne owns — the name is a farmer's honest astonishment, and House Allyrion's gilded hand on its banners has held the river-junction for centuries under a motto that is pure border arithmetic: No Foe May Pass. Wheat, olives, citrus and the river trade make the Allyrions wealthy by any kingdom's measure and essential by Dorne's; armies march on the Greenblood's grain, and Sunspear knows precisely whose fields fill the granaries. The house's best-known son in the tale is a bastard with a knight's fame — the Bastard of Godsgrace, once squire to the Red Viper and rather more than that to a princess of Dorne, whose name surfaces wherever the books' Dornish intrigues need a sword that can be trusted. The river runs green past the walls, the hand on the banners stays open, and the foe, to date, has not passed.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:vaith": {
    paras: [
      "Vaith takes its name from the river it sits on — or gave its name to it; the family and the geography have been entangled too long for anyone to swear which came first — where the red sands begin in earnest and the Greenblood's southern branch is the only argument against them. House Vaith's lordship is the classic desert-edge holding: date palms and irrigation where the river consents, red emptiness where it does not, and a seat whose value is the crossing itself, the last water on the road into the deep sand. Its lords are counted among the desert Dornish, the spear-first branch of the principality's temperament, and the tale keeps them where geography put them — between Sunspear and the sand, holding the door the desert knocks on.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:lemonwood": {
    paras: [
      "Lemonwood scents its stretch of the lower Greenblood — groves of the trees that named it running down toward the river's mouth and the orphans' Planky Town, where Dorne's river folk lash their boats into a floating market. House Dalt has held the orchards since the Rhoynar came, lemons on purple flying over a house of knights rather than power; its best blade in the tale is the Knight of Lemonwood, a name that sounds like a jape until you see the man use a lance, and Dalts ride in Martell escorts and Martell plots wherever the books send the principality's trusted swords. It is Dorne at its most Rhoynish — river, trade, orchard, and the sunniest lordship in a story that mostly grows darker fruit.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:salt-shore": {
    paras: [
      "Salt Shore faces the Summer Sea on Dorne's southern coast, where the trade winds come up from the Stepstones and the Summer Isles, and House Gargalen — a red cockatrice on its banners, wings spread over the door — has held the salt pans, the harborage and the long white beaches since before the Rhoynar landed. The salty Dornish of the coasts are the principality's easiest company, sea traders with Rhoynish manners and old money, and the Gargalens fit the type: their lord is a fixture of Sunspear's councils, dry of wit and close to the Martells, and his seat's contribution to Dorne's wars has always been the coast itself — ships provisioned, landings denied, and salt, which armies forget they need until they are dying for it. The sea does the shouting at Salt Shore; the house has never had to.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= Landmarks =================

  "loc:the-kingswood": {
    paras: [
      "The kingswood spreads south of the Blackwater in a green sweep from the river to the marches of the stormlands — the crown's own forest, where kings have hunted boar and stag since the Targaryens fenced it for their pleasure, and where everyone else hunts at the risk of a hand. Royal forests breed outlaws the way ponds breed frogs, and the kingswood bred the most famous band of them all: the Kingswood Brotherhood, whose chiefs and lieutenants — a would-be robber knight, the terrifying Smiling Knight, a white fawn who ransomed squires — ran the woods until the old Kingsguard came in force. That campaign is a hinge of the backstory: it was in the kingswood that a fifteen-year-old Jaime Lannister fought well enough for Ser Arthur Dayne to knight him on the spot, the last unstained morning of the realm's most argued-over career.",
      "In the tale itself the wood keeps working for the plot in its old capacities — hunting ground, ambush country, and the green route armies take when the roseroad is watched. It is in the kingswood that Robert Baratheon meets his boar, with a squire keeping the wineskin helpfully full; through its eaves that the war of the five kings maneuvers before the Blackwater; and beneath its branches that the story stages its quieter dramas of flight and pursuit, because a royal forest is the one place in the Crownlands where the crown cannot quite see. The smallfolk of its fringes could have told any of these grandees what the wood teaches: kings name forests, but forests outlast the naming.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:the-blackwater-rush": {
    paras: [
      "The Blackwater Rush is the deep, fast river that drains the Gods Eye down to the sea at King's Landing, and its mouth decided where Westeros would keep its capital: Aegon landed there, built there, and three centuries of the realm's traffic have flowed up the bay to his city ever since. It is not a companionable river — swift, dark and bridgeless for hundreds of miles, crossable only by ferry and ford — and its stubbornness shapes every war that touches the capital, because an army on the wrong bank of the Rush is an army with a problem.",
      "Its name belongs above all to one green night. When Stannis Baratheon's fleet came up the bay, the river burned — wildfire seeded ahead of the ships, ignited into a wall of emerald flame that ate hulls, men and the king's hopes together, while the pyromancers' work lit the city walls like a second dawn. The battle of the Blackwater is the tale's greatest set-piece of fire and water: the capital saved at the last hour by a Tyrell-Lannister host out of the dark, a dwarf's unthanked engineering, and a river that did exactly what the defenders paid it to. The Rush has run black and ordinary ever since, carrying trade past the rebuilt quays — but sailors old enough to remember still say you can dredge up green glass candles of the stuff from the bottom, and nobody swims there for pleasure.",
    ],
    vs: [
      "The tellings fight the battle differently. The show gives the wildfire to a single hulk, detonated by one flaming arrow into a fleet-killing blast, and lets Stannis reach the walls with ladders; the books make it a system — the pyromancers' fruits seeded through expendable ships, and above all Tyrion's great boom chain, raised across the river's mouth behind Stannis's fleet so that the burning ships and the trapped ones became the same list. The chain is the books' whole point about the battle: the city was saved less by fire than by arithmetic, a smith's project mocked for weeks closing like a trap at the one moment it mattered. The show, having no chain, gives the arithmetic to a bigger explosion — cheaper to film, and admittedly the greenest thing television ever burned.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:the-mander": {
    paras: [
      "The Mander is the Reach's slow silver spine — the broadest river in Westeros, rising near the Blackwater's headwaters and looping unhurried past Bitterbridge, Longtable, Cider Hall and Highgarden itself before spending into the sea among the Shield Islands. Its valley is the realm's granary: the wheat, fruit and wine that feed King's Landing and half the kingdoms travel its barges, and the tale's power politics keep rediscovering the same fact from new directions — Renly's host eats because it camps on the Mander, Margaery arrives in the capital with the river's bounty in wagons, and every faction that courts Highgarden is really courting the water.",
      "A river that rich has always drawn the wrong sails. The ironborn have come reaving up the Mander since the age of the old kings — the Shield Islands at its mouth were fortified and manned for precisely that reason, four rocks of vigilance guarding the soft green interior — and in the books the old nightmare returns on schedule: a new-crowned king of the isles takes the Shields in a stroke, and suddenly longships row where the wine barges went, deep in the garden of Westeros. The Mander's lesson is the Reach's own, learned and relearned: wealth that flows is wealth that can be boarded, and the realm's gentlest river has needed guarding more than most of its castles.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:the-honeywine": {
    paras: [
      "The Honeywine is the modest river with the sweetest name in Westeros, flowing down out of the northern hills past Honeyholt's hives and Uplands' vineyards to reach the sea through the middle of Oldtown, where it becomes the loveliest urban water in the realm — spanned by stone bridges, walled with guildhalls and sept-fronts, reflecting the Hightower's beacon by night and the Citadel's domes by day. The maesters cross it daily on their way between lectures; the acolytes fish it, against the rules; and the honey-colored light of its valley has been praised in more bad Oldtown verse than any other feature of the Reach, the river being conveniently easier to rhyme than the Citadel.",
      "It has run red exactly when history required. In the Dance of Dragons the Honeywine gave its name to a battle upriver, where an Oldtown host caught between converging enemies was saved by a boy prince on a blue dragon — the day that made the youngest of the era's dragonriders a legend called the Daring. The tale's present asks quieter service of it: the river carries Oldtown's trade, the Citadel's visitors, and, in the books' late chapters, a swelling traffic of refugees and rumors from the coasts the ironborn are burning, all funneling toward the city at the river's mouth. The Honeywine has watered the realm's memory-keepers for a thousand years; it is about to find out, readers suspect, what the price of that is.",
    ],
  },

});
